[{"query":"Modern Awards National Employment Standards NES knowledge verification scoring policy Melbourne Business Services","method":"BM25 keyword search","results":[{"chunk_id":"POL-PURPOSE","source":"Melbourne Business Services - Recruitment and Selection Policy (fictional)","section":"1 Purpose and scope; 2 Roles","title":"Purpose of the policy and who makes hiring decisions","score":16.47,"text":"1.1 Melbourne Business Services (MBS) selects staff on merit, by assessing each applicant's evidence against the selection criteria published in the job advertisement. Nothing else is assessed.

1.2 This policy applies to every stage of recruitment, including screening done with software or artificial intelligence (AI).

2.1 The Recruiter for the vacancy is accountable for the shortlist. The Hiring Manager makes the final appointment decision.

2.2 Screening tools, including the AI pre-screening workflow, provide decision support only. A tool may recommend, but it may not reject an applicant, appoint an applicant or contact an applicant. Every decision not to progress an applicant must be confirmed by the Recruiter, who records the reason.

2.3 The People & Culture Manager owns this policy and must approve any change to screening criteria, prompts or scoring rules used by screening tools."},{"chunk_id":"POL-OVERSEAS","source":"Melbourne Business Services - Recruitment and Selection Policy (fictional)","section":"4.4 Overseas qualifications and experience; names and language background","title":"Overseas qualifications, overseas experience, names and language background","score":16.38,"text":"4.4.1 Overseas qualifications and experience are assessed on whether their content and level are equivalent to the criterion, not on the country where they were gained.

4.4.2 "Australian experience" is not a criterion for the Payroll Officer role. Knowledge of Australian Modern Awards and the National Employment Standards is a separate desirable criterion (D2) and is scored only on stated evidence of that knowledge.

4.4.3 If equivalence of an overseas qualification cannot be judged from the application, the assessor records "information missing: qualification equivalence" and refers to the Recruiter. The applicant is not scored as having no qualification.

4.4.4 An applicant's name, country of birth, accent or language background must not influence any score. Written communication is assessed only if it is a stated criterion."},{"chunk_id":"EOA-20","source":"Equal Opportunity Act 2010 (Vic)","section":"s 20","title":"Reasonable adjustments for applicants and employees with disability","score":5.96,"text":"Under section 20, if a person offered employment (or an employee) has a disability and needs adjustments to perform the genuine and reasonable requirements of the job, the employer must make reasonable adjustments. The duty does not apply if the person could not adequately perform those requirements even with the adjustments.

The Act's examples of adjustments are providing a ramp or specific software, modifying work instructions or manuals, allowing time off for rehabilitation or treatment, and allowing more frequent breaks.

To decide whether an adjustment is reasonable, the employer considers:
- the person's circumstances and the nature of their disability;
- the nature of the role and of the adjustment;
- the employer's financial circumstances and the size of the business;
- the effect on the workplace;
- the consequences of making, or not making, the adjustment.

The VEOHRC recruitment guideline says this duty applies during recruitment, for example to interview arrangements. It also advises asking every candidate whether they need assistance.

Practical meaning for screening: an adjustment request is information for the recruiter to act on, not a factor in scoring."},{"chunk_id":"EOA-ADS","source":"Equal Opportunity Act 2010 (Vic)","section":"s 182 (discriminatory advertising); VEOHRC Guideline: Recruitment ss 1.5.1, 3.5","title":"Discriminatory job advertisements and selection criteria","score":4.33,"text":"Under section 182, it is an offence to publish or display an advertisement, or to authorise one, that indicates an intention to discriminate. Penalties apply to both organisations and individuals.

The VEOHRC recruitment guideline says advertisements should focus on the skills and abilities the job requires. It warns that phrases such as "young, dynamic team" or "mature, experienced professional" signal an age preference and should be avoided.

When writing selection criteria, the guideline advises employers to:
- clarify the genuine requirements of the job first;
- avoid discriminatory language and irrelevant personal attributes;
- separate essential from desirable criteria and rank them by importance.

Examples of problem criteria in a payroll role:
- "native English speaker": race or national origin proxy;
- "young and energetic": age proxy;
- "recent graduate": age proxy;
- "must have Australian experience": national origin proxy, unless genuinely required;
- "physically fit": disability proxy, unless an inherent requirement with a stated reason.

Such criteria should be removed from scoring and referred to HR to amend the advertisement."}]}]
